# The wolf-lang commit this repo is keyed to. A bump is its own commit, with
# the re-vendored data, and nothing else (vendor/README.md).
#
# le05 pins the RELEASE TAG `v0.2.2`, which points at this sha. le04 pinned
# v0.2.1 the same way; nothing here requires a tag, and the next bump may be a
# trunk sha again.
commit = 8cda3aa41004775bb4a4c0a600d7fb673143b7d0
# The exact `wolf --version` string that commit produces (first line; the
# binary also prints a second line naming its lupin pairing — 0.1.22 at this
# pin). `lspconf doctor` fails on a mismatch: a stale local `wolf` producing
# green transcripts is the failure mode the pin exists to prevent.
#
# D57 decides the shape. A build is a release build only when the builder
# stamps BOTH `WOLF_COMMIT=<short sha>` and `WOLF_RELEASE=v{crate version}`,
# and the second is granted only when that exact tag points at HEAD. This pin
# is at v0.2.2, so a release build prints the BARE version and the pin clause.
# An unstamped build still prints `+dev.unknown` — the string any trunk build
# of the same crate version prints, which is exactly the stale-binary hole —
# so the pinned string remains a stamped build's, and `cargo xtask dist` in
# the submodule is the builder that computes the stamp (README §Running the
# server lane locally; its plain-cargo recipe cannot grant the release half).
#
# `xtask compat`'s `version_number` reads the number out of this string; it
# learned semver build metadata at le03 and takes the bare tag form
# unchanged — `0.2.2`, no `+` to strip. Asserted in xtask/src/compat.rs.
#
# THE PIN CLAUSE IS SEVEN HEX DIGITS HERE, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER TO
# wolf-lang#199, NOT A CHOICE. le04 pinned an EIGHT-digit clause
# (`pin 75fd2d0b`) and wrote down the suspicion; le05 measured it. The width
# is `git rev-parse --short`'s AUTO abbreviation, and git sizes that to the
# object count of the clone it is run in — so it is a property of the
# BUILDER'S CLONE, not of the commit. Measured on nomad-1, 2026-09-02, in a
# clone of 2086 objects: `git rev-parse --short 8cda3aa4…` -> `8cda3aa`, and
# the installed binary prints `pin 8cda3aa` to match. The same commit built
# from a full clone of wolf-lang would print `pin 8cda3aa4` and `doctor`
# would refuse it against this line.
#
# That is not a defect in this file. This file's whole job is to record what
# ONE binary prints and refuse anything else, and it does that correctly. The
# defect is upstream, in D57's clause being clone-dependent at all, and it
# stays filed as wolf-lang#199 rather than papered over here — see
# docs/COMPAT.md, which states the consequence for anyone who builds their
# own `wolf` instead of acquiring the pinned artifact.
version = "wolf 0.2.2 (wolfgang, pin 8cda3aa)"
# Does `wolf lsp` exist at this commit? YES — wolf-lang s52 landed it
# (`wolf: the lsp subcommand`, 07e553d), and s133 added definition, references
# and rename on top. This flip is what lights every server-dependent lane:
# record, replay, onetruth, bench, and the encoding, cancellation, navigation
# and fuzz suites all reach `Availability::Ready` as soon as a binary at this
# version resolves.
#
# The acquire step is dark for a THIRD distinct reason at this pin, and the
# change is the good kind. wolf-lang#200 is resolved: measured 2026-09-02,
# `gh release list --repo wolffe-lang/wolf-lang` shows v0.2.2 as **Latest**,
# not Draft, carrying three tier-1 assets, and an unauthenticated HEAD of
# the release download URL answers 200. The asset exists, it is published,
# and anyone can fetch it.
#
# What does not match is the NAME. `.github/workflows/ci.yml`'s acquire step
# asks for `wolf-<shortsha>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz`; wolf-lang's `xtask dist`
# now publishes `wolf-<version>-<target-triple>.tar.gz` —
# `wolf-0.2.2-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz`,
# `wolf-0.2.2-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz`,
# `wolf-0.2.2-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.tar.gz`. So the lane is no longer dark
# for want of an artifact; it is dark for a stale glob, which is a bug in
# THIS repo and not a fact about upstream. Recorded here, stated in
# docs/MATRIX.md, and filed — le05 does not rewrite the CI lane it cannot
# run, but it refuses to keep calling the lane dark for a reason that
# stopped being true.
#
# One real gap remains upstream: there is no linux/aarch64 archive at this
# tag (wolf-lang trunk 4d9683d repairs it for the next one), so that host
# would still acquire nothing.
serves_lsp = true
